## Part 1 — Recurring Failure Classes (read before debugging)

<!-- A PAT-* class is minted ONLY when a failure has bitten more than once, across more
     than one area. It aggregates incidents rather than recording one, so its shape is
     Title + prose + Watch-for/Signature + Guard, and it enumerates its member incident
     IDs inline. The three below are portable — keep the ones that apply, add your own. -->

### PAT-1 — Empty HTTP-200 body → `res.json()` throws
A service returns HTTP 200 with an **empty body** (not `[]`); `await res.json()` throws.
- **Signature:** `SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input`.
- **Guard:** `const t = await res.text(); return t.trim() ? JSON.parse(t) : []`.

### PAT-2 — Secret leaked into a URL, log line, or commit
A token/key interpolated into a remote URL, echoed to a log, or staged for commit.
- **Watch for:** `https://<token>@…` remotes; `echo $TOKEN`; `.env` in `git status`.
- **Guard:** credential-helper / header auth only; never interpolate secrets into URLs or
  logs; `.gitignore` every secret file (see the Scratch skill). Names + status in docs,
  values never.

### PAT-3 — Knowledge-infra not seeded at scaffold (this registry's charter)
A project scaffolded *without* its knowledge-infra runs its most error-prone phase blind,
and retrofitting costs more than seeding.
- **Guard:** ship the scaffolds pre-seeded from day one — **this hub + per-area error
  files + a triage stub + a state-doc skeleton + a `MEMORY.md` + an SOP index**, carrying
  the portable generic `PAT-*` taxonomy. (See the Memory and Handoff skills.)

